/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: IC-205 Practical Threat Hunting */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["IC-205"] = {
ov:"Threat hunting is the proactive search for adversaries that automated detection missed. This operational course builds the hunting discipline: hypothesis-driven investigation, endpoint and network hunting vectors, SIEM query construction, and turning hunts into durable detections. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus applied scenarios.",
mods:[
{t:"The Threat Hunting Philosophy",d:"Assume breach and go looking.",
obj:["Explain the premise and value of proactive threat hunting","Contrast hunting with alert-driven detection","Explain the assume-breach mindset"],
read:["Threat hunting proceeds from a premise: sophisticated adversaries evade automated detection, so if you only respond to alerts, you never find the intrusions your tools missed — and the most damaging intrusions are precisely the ones that evade tooling and dwell for months (BC-101's dwell-time lesson). Hunting is the proactive, human-driven search for adversaries that have gotten past the automated defenses. It assumes breach — that an adversary may already be present — and goes looking, rather than waiting for an alarm that a capable adversary has ensured will not sound.",
"Hunting complements, not replaces, alert-driven detection. Automated detection catches the known and the noisy; hunting catches the novel and the stealthy, and — crucially — every successful hunt should produce a new automated detection so the same threat is caught automatically next time (the operationalization loop from BC-108). The value is both direct (finding present intrusions) and compounding (improving automated detection over time). Hunting also validates defenses and deepens the hunter's understanding of the environment and the adversary. It is a mature-program capability that presumes solid logging, baselining, and detection are already in place — the foundations from the earlier courses."],
ex:"A SOC that only responds to alerts has never found the APT that has dwelled in its network for eight months, carefully avoiding every automated tripwire. A hunting team, assuming breach and proactively searching for the subtle indicators of such an adversary — anomalous authentication patterns, unusual persistence — finds it. The hunt caught what alert-driven detection, by design, could not: the intrusion sophisticated enough to evade the tools.",
refs:[["SANS, Threat Hunting methodology","The proactive, hypothesis-driven hunting discipline."],["MITRE, TTP-based hunting","Hunting for adversary behavior that evades signatures."]],
kc:[
{q:"The premise of threat hunting is that:",o:["All threats trigger alerts","Sophisticated adversaries evade automated detection, so you must proactively search for what the tools missed","Hunting replaces detection","Alerts are unnecessary"],a:1,x:"Capable adversaries avoid tripwires and dwell for months; hunting proactively finds the intrusions automated detection missed."},
{q:"Every successful hunt should produce:",o:["Only a report","A new automated detection, so the same threat is caught automatically next time","Nothing further","A new firewall"],a:1,x:"Hunting compounds value by turning discoveries into automated detections — the operationalization loop from BC-108."},
{q:"Threat hunting adopts which mindset?",o:["Trust the perimeter","Assume breach — that an adversary may already be present — and go looking","Wait for alerts","Prevention only"],a:1,x:"Hunting assumes an adversary may already be inside and searches proactively, rather than waiting for an alarm that may never sound."}]},
{t:"Hypothesis-Driven Hunting",d:"Starting from a specific, testable idea.",
obj:["Explain hypothesis-driven hunting","Describe forming a good hunting hypothesis","Explain sourcing hypotheses from threat intelligence"],
read:["Effective hunting is hypothesis-driven, not aimless data-staring: the hunter forms a specific, testable idea about how an adversary might be operating in the environment, then examines the data to confirm or refute it. A good hypothesis is grounded (based on real adversary behavior, not fantasy), specific enough to test (if an adversary used technique X, we would see evidence Y in data source Z), and relevant to the environment and its likely threats. This structure gives the hunt direction and a clear success criterion, distinguishing it from unproductive browsing.",
"Hypotheses come from several sources. Threat intelligence (BC-108) is the richest: a report that a group targeting your sector uses a specific persistence technique becomes a hypothesis (are they using it here?). The MITRE ATT&CK matrix provides a catalog of techniques to hypothesize against, especially ones your detection does not cover (the gap analysis from BC-108). The environment itself suggests hypotheses (what would compromise of this crown-jewel system look like?). Anomalies from baselining (BC-102) can seed hypotheses. The discipline is to start every hunt with a clear hypothesis, so that the hunt has a purpose and a definite outcome — confirmed (an intrusion found), refuted (increased assurance), or a new detection built regardless."],
ex:"A threat report states that a group active in the sector establishes persistence via a specific scheduled-task technique. The hunter forms a hypothesis — if this group is present, we would see scheduled tasks with these characteristics in our endpoint data — and queries for it across the environment. The hunt has clear direction and a definite outcome, whether it finds the adversary or increases confidence they are absent. The intelligence-sourced hypothesis gave the hunt purpose.",
refs:[["SANS, hypothesis-driven hunting","Forming and testing hunting hypotheses."],["MITRE ATT&CK","A catalog of techniques to hypothesize and hunt against."]],
kc:[
{q:"A good hunting hypothesis is:",o:["Vague and broad","Grounded in real adversary behavior, specific enough to test, and relevant to the environment","Never tested","Random"],a:1,x:"A testable, grounded, relevant hypothesis gives the hunt direction and a clear success criterion, unlike aimless data-staring."},
{q:"The richest source of hunting hypotheses is:",o:["Random guessing","Threat intelligence — e.g., a report that a sector-relevant group uses a specific technique","The vendor","The weather"],a:1,x:"Threat intelligence turns real adversary behavior into testable hypotheses (are they doing this here?), especially for uncovered techniques."},
{q:"Every hypothesis-driven hunt has a definite outcome:",o:["Only success","Confirmed (intrusion found), refuted (increased assurance), or a new detection built — always a result","Always failure","No outcome"],a:1,x:"The hypothesis structure ensures the hunt produces a definite result and value regardless of whether an adversary is found."}]},
{t:"The Hunting Data Foundation",d:"You can only hunt what you collect.",
obj:["Identify the data sources hunting depends on","Explain the relationship between visibility and hunting capability","Explain data quality and retention for hunting"],
read:["Hunting is only as good as the data available to hunt in — you cannot find evidence of a technique in a data source you do not collect. The foundational data sources are the ones the earlier courses built: rich endpoint telemetry (process execution, command lines, network connections — Sysmon/EDR from BC-103), authentication and directory logs (BC-104), network flow, DNS, and proxy logs (BC-102), and cloud control-plane logs (IC-201). The breadth and depth of this telemetry directly determines what an adversary technique the hunter can hope to find; gaps in visibility are gaps in hunting capability.",
"Data quality and retention matter as much as breadth. Logs must be detailed enough to reveal the technique (process-creation logs without command lines miss much), synchronized in time (BC-110's correlation requirement), centralized and searchable (a SIEM or data lake), and retained long enough to hunt historically — because adversaries dwell for months, hunting often looks back weeks or longer, requiring retention that many organizations lack. The maturity of the hunting program is capped by the maturity of the data foundation, which is why building comprehensive, high-quality, retained, searchable telemetry is prerequisite to serious hunting. The hunt's reach is defined by the data's reach."],
ex:"A hunter forms a strong hypothesis about a credential-dumping technique but finds the environment does not log the process-access events that would reveal it — a visibility gap makes the hypothesis untestable. Meanwhile, in areas with rich endpoint telemetry retained for 90 days, the hunter can look back across the whole dwell window. The data foundation defined what could and could not be hunted, capping the program at the telemetry's reach.",
refs:[["SANS, hunting data sources and visibility","The telemetry hunting depends on."],["MITRE ATT&CK data sources","Which data sources reveal which techniques."]],
kc:[
{q:"Hunting capability is fundamentally limited by:",o:["The number of hunters","The data collected — you cannot find a technique's evidence in a source you do not log","The office size","The vendor"],a:1,x:"Visibility gaps are hunting gaps; the breadth and depth of telemetry define what techniques can be found."},
{q:"Long log retention matters for hunting because:",o:["It saves money","Adversaries dwell for months, so hunting often looks back weeks or longer across the dwell window","Retention is illegal","Recent logs are enough"],a:1,x:"Long dwell times require historical hunting; short retention prevents looking back across the period an adversary was present."},
{q:"Process-creation logs without command lines:",o:["Are ideal for hunting","Miss much — the command line detail is often essential to reveal a technique","Are the same as with command lines","Are unnecessary"],a:1,x:"Data quality (detail like command lines) determines what a technique the hunter can actually detect, not just data presence."}]},
{t:"Endpoint Hunting Vectors",d:"Where adversaries live on hosts.",
obj:["Identify high-value endpoint hunting vectors","Describe hunting for persistence and execution","Explain hunting living-off-the-land techniques"],
read:["Endpoints are where much adversary activity manifests, and certain vectors are especially productive for hunting. Persistence: adversaries must survive reboots, so hunting for anomalous scheduled tasks, services, registry run keys, startup items, and WMI subscriptions (the persistence techniques from BC-103) often surfaces footholds. Execution and process anomalies: unusual parent-child relationships (a document spawning PowerShell, a web server spawning a shell — BC-103), processes from temporary directories, and unsigned binaries in system paths are behavioral tells. Credential access: process access to LSASS, suspicious use of credential-dumping tools.",
"A particularly important endpoint hunting focus is living-off-the-land — adversaries abusing legitimate system tools (PowerShell, WMI, PsExec, certutil, rundll32) to avoid dropping detectable malware. Because these tools are legitimate, their mere presence is not suspicious; hunting requires spotting anomalous use (PowerShell with encoded commands downloading from the internet, certutil used to fetch a payload). This is where the baseline (BC-102) and behavioral analysis matter most — distinguishing legitimate administrative use from malicious abuse of the same tools. Endpoint hunting rewards knowing both what adversaries do (ATT&CK techniques) and what normal looks like in the environment, so the anomalous stands out against the baseline."],
ex:"A hunter looks for living-off-the-land abuse: querying for PowerShell executions with encoded commands that make outbound connections. Most results are benign administrative scripts, but one is a base64-encoded command downloading a payload from an external IP — an adversary using legitimate PowerShell to avoid dropping malware. The hunt found the malicious use hiding among legitimate use of the same tool, which no signature for PowerShell itself could distinguish.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK, Persistence and Execution tactics","Endpoint techniques to hunt for."],["LOLBAS project","Living-off-the-land binaries and their abuse."]],
kc:[
{q:"A productive endpoint hunting vector for footholds is:",o:["Screen brightness","Persistence mechanisms — anomalous scheduled tasks, services, registry run keys, WMI subscriptions","Font settings","Mouse movement"],a:1,x:"Adversaries must persist across reboots; hunting persistence mechanisms often surfaces their footholds."},
{q:"Living-off-the-land techniques are hard to detect because:",o:["They use custom malware","They abuse legitimate system tools, so mere presence is not suspicious — only anomalous use","They are always encrypted","They never execute"],a:1,x:"Legitimate tools (PowerShell, certutil) require spotting anomalous use against the baseline, not just their presence."},
{q:"Distinguishing malicious from legitimate use of the same tool requires:",o:["Blocking the tool entirely","Behavioral analysis against the baseline — encoded commands, unusual downloads, anomalous context","Ignoring it","A signature for the tool"],a:1,x:"Baseline and behavioral analysis distinguish malicious abuse from legitimate administration of the same legitimate tool."}]},
{t:"Network Hunting Vectors",d:"Where adversaries reveal themselves on the wire.",
obj:["Identify high-value network hunting vectors","Describe hunting C2 and exfiltration patterns","Explain hunting with flow and DNS data"],
read:["Network telemetry reveals adversary activity that endpoints may miss, and certain patterns are especially productive. Command-and-control: beaconing (regular, machine-like connection intervals — BC-102), connections to newly-registered or low-reputation domains, and protocol anomalies. Exfiltration: unusual outbound data volumes and directions (a workstation uploading gigabytes — BC-102's baseline deviation), transfers to unexpected destinations, and off-hours activity. Lateral movement: internal connections that deviate from normal patterns (a workstation connecting to servers it never touches, SMB/RDP between peers). DNS tunneling and other covert channels (BC-102).",
"Flow and DNS data are the network hunter's workhorses (BC-102's telemetry). Flow reveals the connection graph — who talked to whom, how much, when — enabling hunts for beaconing (regular intervals), data staging (unusual volumes), and lateral movement (anomalous internal connections), and it survives encryption because it is metadata. DNS is often the richest single source: hunting for high-entropy or unusually long domain names (tunneling), excessive queries to a single domain, and queries to known-bad or newly-registered domains. Network hunting pairs naturally with endpoint hunting — a beaconing pattern found in flow data leads to the host and process making the connections, and the two views together reconstruct the activity (the Diamond-Model pivoting from BC-108)."],
ex:"A hunter analyzes flow data for beaconing and finds a host connecting to an external IP at precise 5-minute intervals overnight — a C2 pattern. Pivoting to endpoint data reveals the process making the connections and its persistence mechanism. The network hunt surfaced the beaconing that endpoint data alone might not have flagged, and pivoting to the endpoint reconstructed the full activity — network and endpoint hunting reinforcing each other.",
refs:[["SANS, network threat hunting","Hunting with flow, DNS, and proxy data."],["MITRE ATT&CK, Command and Control and Exfiltration","Network techniques to hunt for."]],
kc:[
{q:"A productive network hunting vector for C2 is:",o:["Screen resolution","Beaconing — regular machine-like connection intervals to an external destination","CPU temperature","Keyboard layout"],a:1,x:"Beaconing's regular intervals are a hallmark of automated C2 that flow-data hunting can surface."},
{q:"Flow data is valuable for network hunting because it:",o:["Contains full payloads","Reveals the connection graph (who, how much, when) and survives encryption as metadata","Requires no retention","Only shows email"],a:1,x:"Flow metadata enables hunts for beaconing, staging, and lateral movement, and remains useful even when traffic is encrypted."},
{q:"Network and endpoint hunting together:",o:["Are redundant","Reinforce each other — a network beaconing pattern leads to the host and process, reconstructing the activity","Cannot be combined","Conflict"],a:1,x:"Pivoting between network and endpoint views (Diamond-Model logic) reconstructs activity that either alone might miss."}]},
{t:"SIEM and Query Construction",d:"Turning hypotheses into searches.",
obj:["Explain the role of the SIEM in hunting","Describe query construction in common languages","Explain iterative query refinement"],
read:["Hunting is executed through queries against the centralized telemetry, usually in a SIEM or data platform, so query construction is a core hunting skill. Query languages — Splunk's SPL, Microsoft's KQL, Elastic's query DSL and Lucene — let the hunter express a hypothesis as a search: find PowerShell processes with encoded commands making external connections, or hosts with beaconing patterns. Fluency in at least one query language, and the ability to translate a hypothesis into an effective, performant query across large data, is what turns a hunting idea into an actual investigation.",
"Query construction is iterative. A first query is often too broad (thousands of results, mostly benign) or too narrow (missing variations), so the hunter refines — adding conditions to reduce noise, broadening to catch technique variants, aggregating to surface anomalies (grouping by host to find outliers), and joining data sources to enrich results. This iteration is where hunting skill shows: turning a vague hypothesis into a precise query that surfaces the anomalous among the voluminous normal. The hunter also builds a library of reusable queries, and productive queries become the basis for automated detections (module ten), so query craft both executes the hunt and produces the durable detection that captures its value."],
ex:"A hunter's first query for encoded PowerShell returns 4,000 results — mostly legitimate. Iterating, they add conditions (encoded AND makes an external connection AND from a non-admin context), reducing it to 12 results, one of which is the malicious download. The iterative refinement turned an overwhelming result set into a precise hunt that surfaced the real activity — the query-craft skill at the heart of practical hunting.",
refs:[["Splunk SPL / Microsoft KQL / Elastic documentation","SIEM query languages for hunting."],["SANS, SIEM hunting techniques","Query construction and refinement."]],
kc:[
{q:"Query construction in a SIEM turns:",o:["Alerts into incidents","A hunting hypothesis into an actual search across the telemetry","Logs into firewalls","Hunches into policy"],a:1,x:"Queries express the hypothesis as a search; fluency in a query language is core to executing hunts."},
{q:"Query construction is iterative because:",o:["Queries never work","A first query is often too broad or narrow, so the hunter refines to surface the anomalous among the normal","One query always suffices","Iteration is discouraged"],a:1,x:"Refinement — narrowing noise, broadening for variants, aggregating for outliers — turns a vague idea into a precise, productive hunt."},
{q:"Productive hunting queries should:",o:["Be discarded after use","Become the basis for automated detections, capturing the hunt's value durably","Be kept secret","Never be reused"],a:1,x:"Turning a successful query into an automated detection is the operationalization loop that compounds hunting's value."}]},
{t:"Baselining and Anomaly Detection for Hunting",d:"Knowing normal to find abnormal.",
obj:["Explain the role of baselining in hunting","Describe statistical and behavioral anomaly hunting","Explain stack counting and outlier analysis"],
read:["Much hunting reduces to finding the abnormal, which requires knowing the normal — the baselining discipline from BC-102 applied to hunting. A hunter who knows the environment's normal patterns (which hosts talk to which, what processes run where, typical volumes and timing) can spot the deviations that indicate adversary activity. Without this baseline, everything looks equally plausible and the anomalous cannot be distinguished. Building and internalizing the environment's baseline is therefore foundational to effective hunting, especially for the behavioral techniques that signatures cannot catch.",
"Practical anomaly-hunting techniques include stack counting (aggregating a field and looking at the least-common values — the rare parent-process, the unusual service name, the one host doing something no other host does, are often the anomalies worth investigating), outlier analysis (statistically unusual volumes, timing, or frequencies), and least-frequency analysis (the rarest occurrences are often the most interesting, inverting the usual instinct to look at the most common). These techniques surface the needle in the haystack by exploiting the fact that adversary activity, being rare and different, tends to appear as the statistical outlier. Combined with knowledge of adversary techniques (what kind of anomaly to look for), anomaly hunting is a powerful complement to hypothesis-driven hunting."],
ex:"A hunter stack-counts parent processes across the environment and looks at the least-common: almost every host's processes have normal parents, but on three hosts a process was spawned by an unusual parent seen nowhere else — the rare outlier. Investigation reveals it is the adversary's execution technique. Least-frequency analysis surfaced the needle by exploiting that adversary activity is rare and different, appearing as the statistical outlier the common baseline made visible.",
refs:[["SANS, stack counting and least-frequency analysis","Anomaly-hunting techniques."],["NIST SP 800-137 and BC-102 baselining","Establishing normal to find abnormal."]],
kc:[
{q:"Anomaly-based hunting requires knowing the normal because:",o:["Normal is unimportant","Without a baseline, the anomalous (adversary activity) cannot be distinguished from the ordinary","Baselines are illegal","Everything is anomalous"],a:1,x:"Finding the abnormal presupposes knowing the normal; baselining (BC-102) is foundational to behavioral hunting."},
{q:"Stack counting (least-frequency analysis) surfaces anomalies by:",o:["Looking at the most common values","Aggregating a field and examining the least-common values, where rare adversary activity often appears","Ignoring rare values","Counting only totals"],a:1,x:"Adversary activity is rare and different, appearing as the statistical outlier; least-frequency analysis inverts the usual instinct to find it."},
{q:"Anomaly hunting complements hypothesis-driven hunting by:",o:["Replacing it","Surfacing unexpected outliers that no specific hypothesis anticipated, then guiding investigation","Being unrelated","Only using signatures"],a:1,x:"Anomaly techniques catch the unexpected, while hypotheses target specific techniques — together giving broad and deep coverage."}]},
{t:"Investigating and Validating Findings",d:"From anomaly to confirmed intrusion.",
obj:["Explain investigating a hunting lead","Describe distinguishing true positives from benign anomalies","Explain scoping a confirmed finding"],
read:["A hunt surfaces leads — anomalies, matches, outliers — but most leads are benign, so investigation to distinguish true positives from false ones is essential. Investigating a lead means gathering context (what is this host, this process, this connection; is there a legitimate explanation?), pivoting across data sources to build a picture (the Diamond-Model logic — from the anomalous process to its network connections to related hosts), and applying judgment about whether the totality indicates adversary activity or benign unusualness. Many anomalies are simply rare-but-legitimate; the hunter must not cry wolf on every outlier, but also not dismiss the real threat as noise (the triage balance from BC-110).",
"When a lead is validated as a true positive — a confirmed intrusion — the hunt transitions to incident response (BC-110), and scoping becomes critical: the hunter uses the same pivoting and analysis to determine the full extent of the compromise (how many hosts, what did the adversary do, how did they get in) before containment, because a hunt that finds one host of a ten-host intrusion and stops there leaves the adversary present. The hunter's deep familiarity with the environment and the data, built through hunting, makes them well-positioned to scope quickly. Validated findings also feed back: the true positive informs a new detection (module ten), and even refuted leads increase assurance and refine future hunts."],
ex:"A hunt surfaces 12 anomalous PowerShell executions. Investigating each, the hunter finds 11 are legitimate administrative scripts (benign anomalies) and one is a malicious download — a true positive. Pivoting from that host, they scope the intrusion to three compromised hosts before handing off to incident response for containment. The investigation distinguished the real threat from benign unusualness and scoped it fully — neither crying wolf nor missing the intrusion.",
refs:[["SANS, hunting lead investigation","Validating and scoping hunting findings."],["NIST SP 800-61 Rev.2","Transition from hunt to incident response."]],
kc:[
{q:"Most hunting leads are:",o:["Confirmed intrusions","Benign — investigation is needed to distinguish true positives from rare-but-legitimate anomalies","Always malicious","Ignored"],a:1,x:"Anomalies are often legitimately unusual; the hunter investigates to separate real threats from benign outliers, per the triage balance."},
{q:"When a hunting lead is confirmed as an intrusion, the critical next step is:",o:["Immediate broad containment of one host","Scoping the full extent before containment, since finding one host of a multi-host intrusion and stopping leaves the adversary present","Ignoring it","Closing the hunt"],a:1,x:"Scoping the full compromise (like IR analysis) prevents partial containment that leaves the adversary on unscoped hosts."},
{q:"A validated hunting finding should feed back into:",o:["Nothing","A new automated detection — capturing the hunt's value so the threat is caught automatically next time","Only a report","The vendor"],a:1,x:"True positives inform new detections; the operationalization loop compounds hunting's value beyond the single find."}]},
{t:"From Hunt to Detection",d:"Capturing hunting value durably.",
obj:["Explain converting hunts into automated detections","Describe detection engineering from hunting insights","Explain the compounding value of the hunt-to-detection loop"],
read:["The single most important way hunting compounds its value is by converting successful hunts into automated detections — so that the technique found by a human hunt is caught automatically forever after. This closes the loop the whole program depends on: hunting finds what automation missed, and each find improves automation so it misses less. A hunting program that finds intrusions but never builds detections repeats the same manual work; one that systematically turns hunts into detections continuously raises the automated baseline, freeing hunters to search for the next novel technique.",
"Detection engineering from hunting insights means taking the productive query or the identified technique and building a robust, tuned automated detection — one that fires on the malicious pattern with acceptable false positives (the tuning discipline from BC-102 to avoid alert fatigue), ideally built on durable behavioral indicators (the top of the Pyramid of Pain from BC-108) rather than perishable ones. The best detections come from real hunts because they are grounded in actual adversary behavior observed in the real environment. Over time, this hunt-to-detection loop transforms the SOC: the automated detection coverage (mapped to ATT&CK, per BC-108) steadily expands, driven by hunting, so the organization catches more automatically and hunts for progressively more sophisticated threats. This is how a mature detection program is actually built — not bought, but grown through hunting."],
ex:"A hunt discovers an adversary's living-off-the-land technique. Rather than just remediating, the team builds a tuned automated detection for that behavioral pattern, mapped to the relevant ATT&CK technique, and deploys it to the SIEM. The next time any adversary uses that technique, it is caught automatically — the human hunt became permanent automated coverage. Systematically repeated, the hunt-to-detection loop steadily expanded the SOC's automated detection, the way mature detection is actually grown.",
refs:[["SANS, detection engineering from hunting","Converting hunts into automated detections."],["MITRE ATT&CK and Pyramid of Pain","Building durable, technique-based detections."]],
kc:[
{q:"The most important way hunting compounds its value is:",o:["Writing longer reports","Converting successful hunts into automated detections, so the technique is caught automatically forever after","Hunting the same thing repeatedly","Keeping findings secret"],a:1,x:"The hunt-to-detection loop raises the automated baseline, so automation misses less and hunters pursue the next novel threat."},
{q:"Detections built from real hunts are strong because they are:",o:["Theoretical","Grounded in actual adversary behavior observed in the real environment","Untested","Signature-only"],a:1,x:"Real-hunt detections reflect genuine adversary behavior in the actual environment, ideally on durable behavioral indicators."},
{q:"A hunting program that never builds detections:",o:["Is most efficient","Repeats the same manual work, never raising the automated baseline","Is best practice","Needs no data"],a:1,x:"Without the hunt-to-detection loop, hunting does not compound; mature detection is grown through systematically capturing hunts."}]},
{t:"Threat-Informed and ATT&CK-Based Hunting",d:"Prioritizing hunts by real threats.",
obj:["Explain threat-informed hunt prioritization","Describe using ATT&CK for hunt coverage and planning","Explain purple teaming to validate detection"],
read:["With limited hunting capacity, prioritization matters: threat-informed hunting focuses hunts on the techniques your actual threats use, rather than every conceivable technique. Threat intelligence (BC-108) identifies which groups target your sector and what they do, and hunts prioritize those TTPs. This is the intelligence-driven defense principle applied to hunting — concentrate finite hunting effort where the real adversaries operate. MITRE ATT&CK structures this: hunters map their detection and hunting coverage to the ATT&CK matrix, identify the high-priority uncovered techniques (relevant to their threats), and plan hunts to close those gaps.",
"Purple teaming validates and extends this. In a purple-team exercise, a red team executes specific ATT&CK techniques while the blue team (hunters and detection) checks whether they can find them — directly testing detection and hunting coverage against real techniques. This reveals exactly which techniques are and are not detected, driving both new detections and refined hunts. Combined with ATT&CK coverage mapping, purple teaming turns hunt planning from guesswork into a deliberate program: know your threats' techniques, map your coverage, hunt and build detection for the gaps, and validate with purple-team exercises. This threat-informed, ATT&CK-structured, purple-validated approach is how mature hunting programs allocate their effort for maximum defensive impact — the operationalization of CTI (BC-108) into proactive defense."],
ex:"A hunting team maps its coverage to ATT&CK and finds strong detection of initial-access techniques but gaps in the credential-access and lateral-movement techniques their sector's primary adversary is known to use. They prioritize hunts for those specific techniques, and a purple-team exercise executing them confirms which are caught and which slip through — driving new detections for the gaps. Threat-informed, ATT&CK-structured hunting focused finite effort exactly where the real adversary operates.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK and ATT&CK Navigator","Coverage mapping and hunt planning."],["MITRE, purple teaming and adversary emulation","Validating detection and hunting coverage."]],
kc:[
{q:"Threat-informed hunting prioritizes hunts by:",o:["Alphabetical technique order","The techniques your actual threats use, concentrating finite effort where real adversaries operate","Random selection","The newest technique only"],a:1,x:"Intelligence-driven prioritization focuses hunting where the real threats are, rather than spreading effort over every possibility."},
{q:"MITRE ATT&CK helps hunt planning by:",o:["Blocking attacks","Letting hunters map coverage, identify high-priority uncovered techniques, and plan hunts to close gaps","Encrypting data","Writing queries automatically"],a:1,x:"ATT&CK coverage mapping turns hunt planning into deliberate gap-closing against relevant techniques."},
{q:"A purple-team exercise validates hunting by:",o:["Only attacking","Having a red team execute techniques while the blue team checks whether they can detect them, revealing coverage gaps","Ignoring detection","Replacing hunting"],a:1,x:"Purple teaming directly tests detection/hunting coverage against real techniques, driving new detections for what slips through."}]},
{t:"Hunt Documentation and Metrics",d:"Making hunting a measurable program.",
obj:["Explain documenting hunts for repeatability and knowledge","Describe meaningful hunting metrics","Explain building a mature hunting program"],
read:["Hunting must be documented to be a program rather than heroics. Each hunt is recorded — the hypothesis, the data sources and queries used, the findings (positive or negative), and the outcome (detections built, intrusions found, assurance gained). This documentation makes hunts repeatable (a productive hunt can be re-run periodically), builds institutional knowledge (a library of hunts and queries), and enables handoff and collaboration. Undocumented hunting depends on individuals and evaporates when they leave; documented hunting becomes a durable organizational capability.",
"Hunting metrics measure the program's value and maturity, and — like all security metrics (BC-101) — should focus on outcomes, not activity. Useful metrics include detections created from hunts (the compounding value), intrusions or gaps found, coverage expansion (ATT&CK techniques newly hunted or detected), and dwell-time reduction. Vanity metrics (number of hunts run, queries executed) measure activity, not impact. A mature hunting program is threat-informed, hypothesis-driven, well-documented, measured by outcomes, and continuously feeding the hunt-to-detection loop — a deliberate, improving capability rather than occasional ad hoc searching. Building this maturity, on the foundation of good data and detection, is the destination this course points toward, and it connects hunting to the overall program's measurable resilience."],
ex:"A hunting team documents each hunt in a repository — hypothesis, queries, findings, detections built — and reports quarterly metrics: 8 hunts run, 14 new detections created, 2 previously-undetected intrusions found, and 6 new ATT&CK techniques now covered. The documentation made hunts repeatable and the knowledge durable, while the outcome metrics showed real defensive impact — a mature, measurable program rather than individual heroics.",
refs:[["SANS, hunting program maturity and metrics","Documenting and measuring hunting."],["NIST SP 800-55 Rev.2","Outcome-focused security measurement."]],
kc:[
{q:"Documenting hunts is important because it:",o:["Wastes time","Makes hunts repeatable, builds institutional knowledge, and turns hunting into a durable capability rather than individual heroics","Is never needed","Slows hunting"],a:1,x:"Documentation makes hunting a program — repeatable, transferable, and durable — rather than dependent on individuals."},
{q:"A meaningful hunting metric is:",o:["Number of hunts run","Detections created from hunts, intrusions found, and coverage expansion — outcomes, not activity","Number of queries executed","Hours spent"],a:1,x:"Outcome metrics (detections built, threats found, coverage gained) measure impact; activity counts measure effort, not value."},
{q:"A mature hunting program is:",o:["Occasional ad hoc searching","Threat-informed, hypothesis-driven, documented, outcome-measured, and continuously feeding the hunt-to-detection loop","Purely automated","Dependent on one person"],a:1,x:"Maturity means a deliberate, improving, measured capability on a foundation of good data and detection — not ad hoc heroics."}]},
{t:"Memory and Deep Host Hunting",d:"Finding what disk and logs miss.",
obj:["Explain the value of memory analysis in hunting","Describe hunting for in-memory and fileless threats","Explain integrating deep host analysis into hunts"],
read:["Some adversary activity lives only in memory and never touches disk or produces obvious log entries — fileless malware, injected code, and in-memory-only tooling deliberately avoid the artifacts that endpoint logs and disk forensics capture. Memory analysis (examining a host's RAM) reveals these: injected code in legitimate processes, in-memory-only payloads, unpacked malware, and the true state of a running system that on-disk analysis misses. For hunting the most sophisticated threats — which specifically use fileless techniques to evade detection — memory analysis is often the only way to find them, making it a powerful deep-dive technique when a hunt or a lead points to a specific host.",
"Deep host hunting integrates memory analysis with the endpoint telemetry from earlier modules. A hunt that surfaces a suspicious host (an anomalous process, a beaconing connection) can escalate to capturing and analyzing that host's memory to confirm and understand the threat — finding the injected code behind the anomalous behavior, extracting the malware's configuration, or revealing tooling that left no disk trace. Modern EDR increasingly provides memory-inspection capabilities, and dedicated memory-forensics tools (Volatility) enable deep analysis. This connects to the forensics discipline of IC-208 — memory analysis is a shared skill — and it extends the hunter's reach to the in-memory realm where the most evasive adversaries operate, the deepest layer of the host that fileless threats rely on being invisible."],
ex:"A hunt surfaces a host with a beaconing connection but no suspicious process on disk or in standard logs — the malware is fileless, living in injected memory. Capturing and analyzing the host's memory reveals code injected into a legitimate process, and extracting it yields the C2 configuration. Memory analysis found the fileless threat that disk and log hunting could not see — the deep-host reach for the most evasive adversaries.",
refs:[["Volatility and memory forensics documentation","Analyzing memory for in-memory and fileless threats."],["SANS FOR508, memory hunting","Deep host analysis in hunting and forensics."]],
kc:[
{q:"Memory analysis is essential for hunting because it reveals:",o:["Only disk files","Fileless and in-memory-only threats — injected code, unpacked malware — that disk and logs miss","Network traffic","Nothing new"],a:1,x:"Sophisticated adversaries use fileless techniques specifically to avoid disk and log artifacts; memory analysis finds them."},
{q:"A hunt surfacing a beaconing host with no suspicious process on disk suggests:",o:["A false positive always","A fileless/in-memory threat, warranting memory analysis to find injected code","Nothing to investigate","A disk failure"],a:1,x:"Beaconing with no disk artifact points to a fileless threat living in injected memory — memory analysis is the way to find it."},
{q:"Deep host hunting via memory analysis connects hunting to:",o:["Physical security","The forensics discipline (IC-208) — memory analysis is a shared deep-dive skill","Marketing","Networking only"],a:1,x:"Memory analysis extends the hunter's reach into the in-memory realm and shares skills with digital forensics."}]},
{t:"Cloud and Identity Hunting",d:"Hunting where the modern perimeter is.",
obj:["Explain hunting in cloud environments","Describe identity-focused hunting","Explain adapting hunting to the control plane"],
read:["As environments move to cloud and identity becomes the perimeter (IC-201, BC-104), hunting must follow. Cloud hunting focuses on the control-plane logs (IC-201) — hunting for anomalous API activity, unusual identity behavior, privilege escalations, new access keys or identities (persistence), and access from unusual locations or contexts. The techniques adapt: instead of hunting processes on hosts, the cloud hunter hunts API calls and identity actions, applying the same hypothesis-driven, anomaly-based, threat-informed approach to a different data source (the control plane) using ATT&CK for Cloud (IC-201).",
"Identity-focused hunting is increasingly central because identity attacks (BC-104) are a primary vector across cloud and hybrid environments. Hunters look for anomalous authentication patterns (impossible travel, unusual times, new devices), suspicious privilege changes, dormant-account activation, and the signatures of credential attacks (password spraying, token abuse) — the identity-threat-detection discipline from BC-104 applied proactively. Because a single compromised identity can traverse cloud and on-premises (the hybrid pivot from IC-201), identity hunting spans the whole environment. The hunter's toolkit — hypothesis, data, queries, anomaly analysis, investigation, detection — carries over, but the data sources and techniques adapt to where the modern adversary operates: the cloud control plane and the identity layer, not just the traditional host and network."],
ex:"A cloud hunter hypothesizes that an adversary who obtained a leaked key would create a new identity for persistence, and queries control-plane logs for identity-creation events from unusual contexts. Finding one — a new admin identity created from an unfamiliar location shortly after anomalous API activity — reveals a cloud intrusion. The same hunting discipline, applied to control-plane and identity data, found the adversary operating where the modern perimeter actually is.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK for Cloud","Cloud adversary techniques to hunt."],["Cloud provider and identity threat-hunting guidance","Hunting control-plane and identity telemetry."]],
kc:[
{q:"Cloud hunting focuses primarily on:",o:["Physical servers","Control-plane logs — anomalous API activity, identity behavior, and privilege escalations","Screen resolution","Disk sectors"],a:1,x:"In cloud, hunting shifts from host processes to API calls and identity actions in the control plane."},
{q:"Identity-focused hunting looks for:",o:["Font changes","Anomalous authentication (impossible travel, new devices), suspicious privilege changes, and credential-attack signatures","CPU temperature","Cable faults"],a:1,x:"Identity attacks are a primary vector; hunting authentication and privilege anomalies proactively finds them across cloud and hybrid."},
{q:"The hunting discipline transfers to cloud and identity by:",o:["Being abandoned","Keeping the same hypothesis-driven, anomaly-based, threat-informed approach while adapting data sources and techniques","Using only signatures","Ignoring the control plane"],a:1,x:"The hunter's toolkit carries over; only the data sources (control plane, identity) and specific techniques adapt to where adversaries operate."}]},
{t:"Deception and Active Defense in Hunting",d:"Making adversaries reveal themselves.",
obj:["Explain how deception supports hunting","Describe honeypots, honeytokens, and canaries","Explain the high signal value of deception"],
read:["Deception turns the environment into a hunting tool by planting assets that only an adversary would touch, so any interaction is a high-confidence signal (the deception technique from BC-101). Honeypots (decoy systems), honeytokens (fake credentials, files, or database entries), and canary tokens (tripwire files or URLs) sit in the environment doing nothing until an adversary — exploring, harvesting credentials, or moving laterally — interacts with them, immediately revealing their presence and often their location and technique. Because legitimate users have no reason to touch these decoys, the false-positive rate is near zero, making a tripped decoy one of the highest-signal alerts that exists.",
"For hunting, deception provides both detection and direction. A tripped honeytoken (a fake credential used, a canary file accessed) is a confirmed lead that immediately focuses the hunt — the adversary is here, doing this — inverting the usual needle-in-haystack problem by making the adversary announce themselves. Deception is especially valuable against the sophisticated, evasive adversaries that hunting targets: even an adversary careful enough to evade all detection may take the bait of a plausible-looking credential or an enticing file. Integrating deception (canary tokens in file shares, honeytokens in the directory, decoy systems in segments) into the environment gives hunters high-signal tripwires throughout, complementing the proactive searching with traps that make adversaries reveal themselves — active defense in support of the hunt."],
ex:"A hunting team plants honeytokens — fake admin credentials in a place an adversary harvesting credentials would find them, and canary files in sensitive shares. Weeks later, one of the fake credentials is used to authenticate. Because no legitimate process would ever use it, this is a near-certain adversary signal, and it immediately focuses the hunt on the source. The deception made the evasive adversary announce themselves — a high-signal tripwire complementing proactive hunting.",
refs:[["MITRE Engage","Planning deception and adversary engagement."],["Canary token / honeypot documentation","Deception assets for high-signal detection."]],
kc:[
{q:"Deception assets (honeytokens, canaries) produce high-signal alerts because:",o:["They are encrypted","Legitimate users have no reason to touch them, so any interaction is near-certain adversary activity","They are loud","They block attacks"],a:1,x:"Near-zero false positives make a tripped decoy one of the highest-confidence signals available — a core deception property."},
{q:"For hunting, a tripped honeytoken provides:",o:["Only noise","A confirmed lead that immediately focuses the hunt — the adversary announces their presence","A false positive","Nothing useful"],a:1,x:"Deception inverts the needle-in-haystack problem by making adversaries reveal themselves, focusing the hunt directly."},
{q:"Deception is especially valuable against:",o:["No one","Sophisticated, evasive adversaries — even those who evade all detection may take the bait","Only script kiddies","Legitimate users"],a:1,x:"An adversary careful enough to evade detection may still take a plausible bait, which is why deception complements hunting for evasive threats."}]},
{t:"Threat Hunting Capstone",d:"Running a full hunt from hypothesis to detection.",
obj:["Sequence a complete hunt end to end","Integrate hypothesis, data, queries, investigation, and detection","Judge hunting by intrusions found and detections built"],
read:["This capstone runs a complete hunt. Starting from a threat-informed hypothesis (module two) — a sector-relevant adversary's technique, mapped to ATT&CK (module eleven) — the hunter confirms the necessary data is available (module three), constructs and iteratively refines queries against endpoint and network telemetry (modules four through seven), applies anomaly techniques like stack counting to surface leads (module eight), investigates the leads to distinguish true positives from benign anomalies and scope any confirmed intrusion (module nine), builds an automated detection from the productive hunt (module ten), and documents the whole hunt with its outcomes (module twelve). Each step builds on the last, from idea to durable defensive improvement.",
"The integration is the point: a hunt is not a single query but a disciplined process combining intelligence, data, query craft, investigation, and detection engineering. A hunter who has good hypotheses but poor data cannot execute; one with data but no query skill cannot search; one who finds anomalies but cannot investigate cries wolf or misses threats; one who finds intrusions but builds no detections does not compound value. The capstone requires all of these together, which is why hunting is a mature-program capability that presumes the foundations from every earlier course — logging, baselining, detection, incident response, and threat intelligence.",
"The graded deliverable — forming a hunting hypothesis, running advanced queries across an endpoint data lake, and uncovering an unalerted web shell — is exactly this end-to-end hunt. Its quality is judged by whether the hunt found real adversary activity that automated detection missed and produced a durable detection to catch it next time — the twin measures of hunting value. An organization with mature hunting continuously finds and closes the gaps in its automated defenses, steadily shrinking the space in which sophisticated adversaries can operate undetected — the proactive edge of the resilience posture this academy builds.",],
ex:"A hunter runs the full capstone hunt: a threat-informed hypothesis about a web-shell technique, queries across the endpoint data lake refined to surface anomalous web-server child processes, investigation confirming one is a real unalerted web shell, scoping the compromise, and a new automated detection built for the technique — all documented. The hunt found what automated detection missed and produced permanent coverage — the twin measures of hunting value, achieved through the integrated discipline.",
refs:[["SANS FOR508/FOR572 hunting methodology","End-to-end threat hunting."],["MITRE ATT&CK and detection engineering","Hunt-to-detection across the full process."]],
kc:[
{q:"A complete hunt integrates:",o:["Only a single query","Intelligence, data, query craft, investigation, and detection engineering as a disciplined end-to-end process","Just a report","Only automated tools"],a:1,x:"Hunting is a process combining hypothesis, data, queries, investigation, and detection — not an isolated query."},
{q:"Hunting is a mature-program capability because it presumes:",o:["Nothing","The foundations from earlier courses — logging, baselining, detection, IR, and threat intelligence","Only a scanner","No prior capability"],a:1,x:"Effective hunting depends on solid telemetry, baselining, detection, and intelligence already being in place."},
{q:"Hunting value is judged by:",o:["Number of hunts","Real adversary activity found that automation missed, and durable detections built to catch it next time","Query count","Hours logged"],a:1,x:"The twin measures are intrusions found and detections built — finding what tools missed and compounding the value durably."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: A SOC only responds to alerts and never finds an APT that dwelled eight months evading every tripwire. The lesson is:",o:["Alerts are enough","Hunting is needed — sophisticated adversaries evade automated detection, so you must proactively search","Buy more tools","The APT was lucky"],a:1,x:"The most damaging intrusions evade tooling; only proactive, assume-breach hunting finds what automated detection missed."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A hunter wants to start a hunt but has no specific idea, just a vague feeling. The correct approach is:",o:["Stare at dashboards","Form a specific, testable, threat-informed hypothesis to give the hunt direction and a clear outcome","Run random queries","Skip the hunt"],a:1,x:"Hypothesis-driven hunting gives direction and a definite result; aimless data-staring is unproductive."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A strong hypothesis about credential dumping cannot be tested because the environment does not log process-access events. This shows:",o:["The hypothesis was bad","Hunting capability is capped by the data collected — visibility gaps are hunting gaps","Hunting is impossible","The hunter lacked skill"],a:1,x:"You cannot find a technique's evidence in a source you do not log; the data foundation defines what can be hunted."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A hunt for encoded PowerShell returns 4,000 mostly-benign results. The skilled response is:",o:["Give up","Iteratively refine the query (encoded AND external connection AND non-admin context) to surface the real activity","Investigate all 4,000","Ignore the hunt"],a:1,x:"Query refinement turns an overwhelming result set into a precise hunt that surfaces the anomalous among the normal."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Stack-counting parent processes reveals three hosts with a parent seen nowhere else. This is:",o:["Noise to ignore","A least-frequency outlier worth investigating — adversary activity is rare and different","Always benign","A false positive by definition"],a:1,x:"Adversary activity appears as the statistical outlier; least-frequency analysis surfaces the needle in the haystack."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A hunt confirms a compromised host. Before containment, the hunter should:",o:["Contain that one host and stop","Scope the full extent of the intrusion, since finding one host of a multi-host compromise and stopping leaves the adversary present","Close the hunt","Ignore other hosts"],a:1,x:"Scoping before containment (like IR analysis) prevents partial containment that leaves the adversary on unscoped hosts."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A hunting team finds intrusions but never builds detections, repeating the same manual searches. The missing discipline is:",o:["More hunters","The hunt-to-detection loop — converting hunts into automated detections to compound value","More data","A bigger SIEM"],a:1,x:"Without capturing hunts as detections, hunting does not raise the automated baseline; the loop is how mature detection is grown."},
{q:"SCENARIO: With limited capacity, a team must choose which techniques to hunt. The right basis is:",o:["Every conceivable technique equally","Threat-informed prioritization — the techniques the sector's actual adversaries use, mapped to ATT&CK coverage gaps","Alphabetical order","The newest technique"],a:1,x:"Finite hunting effort should concentrate where real threats operate, guided by intelligence and ATT&CK coverage mapping."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A purple-team exercise runs specific ATT&CK techniques and finds several are undetected. The value is:",o:["Proving the red team is skilled","Revealing exact coverage gaps, driving new detections and refined hunts for the techniques that slipped through","Embarrassing the blue team","Nothing"],a:1,x:"Purple teaming validates coverage against real techniques, turning hunt planning into deliberate gap-closing."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A hunting program is judged mature. The correct evidence is:",o:["Number of hunts run","Threat-informed, documented, outcome-measured hunting that continuously feeds the hunt-to-detection loop","A large SIEM","Many queries"],a:1,x:"Maturity is a deliberate, documented, outcome-driven capability that compounds via hunt-to-detection — not activity counts."}
]};
